fix(capture): address the security and durability review findings

Six review findings on the extension capture path:

- A page could forge a capture: the extractor mirrored its result into the
  page's DOM as a fallback, and a page could plant that node to make the
  extension save a URL of its choosing. The result now travels only over the
  isolated world, which page script cannot reach. The DOM fallback existed
  only as belt-and-braces for a handoff that already works, so it is deleted
  rather than authenticated — a nonce would narrow the window without closing
  it, because the page can read the node as soon as it appears.
- The server moved to a new port when its saved one was taken, while the
  extension kept posting the bearer token to the old address. It now refuses
  to start and reports "not running" instead.
- Selection, image, and video captures are queued when the app is closed,
  like page captures. The queue only understood page payloads, so a media
  save was silently dropped.
- Video oEmbed enrichment only fills fields the capture originally stored,
  so a user rename made before the response lands survives.
- Article cards render only the cached local favicon, so opening the library
  no longer contacts the saved site's server.
- A context-menu quote goes through the collector, keeping its markup and
  page title.
- Favicon storage checks the item still exists before writing, so deleting
  an item mid-download no longer leaves an orphan asset directory.
